II. RELATED WORKS
A. Multisensor Data Fusion
To reduce inaccuracy and uncertainty of single sensor measurements, the IoT-based gas concentration monitoring system with multiple sensors is often deployed [2]. In such an IoT system scenario, improved calibration can be achieved by multisensor data fusion, where information from different sensors are combined in the cloud to provide an overall more accurate gas concentration measurement. To deal with the uncertainty caused by the imperfection of the data, different traditional Bayesian probability theory-based approaches have been proposed as data fusion techniques. As a special case of the Bayesian filters, the Kalman filter and its variants have been widely used for sensor fusion in many existing works, such as [20]–[22]. As a generalization to the traditional Bayesian probability theory, the belief function theory (also known as the DS theory) [23] provides a particularly convenient theoretical framework for uncertainty modeling and propagation in the combination of partially reliable information. It has been widely applied to multisensor data fusion, such as [24]–[26]. However, the traditional DS theory will result in unreasonable fused beliefs when the belief functions provided by different sensors highly conflict with each other. To mitigate this issue, preprocessing methods of the original belief functions have been proposed [27]. Murphy [28] proposed to incorporate an average belief into the DS combining rule. However, this simple average approach assigns equal weight to each body of evidence and does not consider the relationship among the evidence collected from multiple sensors, which is often unreasonable in real application. For those cases, different weighted average approaches [29]–[31] have been proposed to manage the conflicts between belief functions and are shown to have good performance for the specific application. In this article, based on the stochastic model we built for the single sensor using HMM, we further propose a general belief function fusion framework for our proposed multisensor fusion-based NDIR gas sensor calibration problem. Then, we propose a modified weighted average approach to deal with the case where belief functions highly conflict with each other. Different from the aforementioned works [29]–[31], which
utilize the Jousselme distance [32] or the modified Jousselme distance as a measure of the distance between the belief functions, we propose to use the Wasserstein distance [33] as a measure of distance between the belief functions. This is done since the Jousselme distance is not applicable for the case where the belief functions are just simple Bayesian probability measures.
B. Deep Reinforcement Learning
The aforementioned multisensor fusion only provides an instantaneous solution for the NDIR gas calibration problem, without considering the overall long-term performance. It is however easy to be implemented since it does not require the statistical relation between sensors, i.e., joint statistics. It will work well in scenarios where the majority of the belief functions provide reliable and correct evidence. Moreover, the fusion result provided by this approach can be used as an approximation of the joint statistics of the system. With this approximated joint statistics, we next formulate the calibration problem as a Markov decision process (MDP) problem [34], where the long-term stable calibration results are derived in a sequential manner. Reinforcement learning provides model-free methods to solve such optimal control problems of dynamic systems without requiring the knowledge of the stochastic properties of the underlying model. Due to this convenience, reinforcement learning methods have been widely applied in sensor calibration problems during the recent years. Cao et al. [35] proposed a Q-learning-based Gaussian mixture model (GMM) algorithm for vehicle tracking via multisensor fusion. Meanwhile, a SARSA [36]-based reinforcement learning sensor fusion algorithm is proposed for autonomous robot navigation in [37]. When combined with deep learning, the more powerful DRL methods can deal with the curse-of-dimensionality problem by approximating the value functions as well as policy functions using deep neural networks. In this article, we reformulate our calibration problem as a stochastic optimal control problem and propose to use a deep Q-network (DQN) to solve it.
III. NDIR SENSOR MECHANISM
The general operation principle of an NDIR sensor is illustrated Fig. 1. When the sensor starts working, the broadband infrared light will be directed through the gas chamber filled by gas from the external environment toward the detector. A general wavelength range of interests for the NDIR sensing is 3–20 μm. However, NDIR sensing does not require light sources and detectors with narrow spectral range of operation. As long as the source and detector cover the wavelength range of interest, i.e., the range containing the target gas absorption band of interest, an NDIR measurement can be implemented. The IR light with different wavelengths is absorbed by gas molecules in the volume probed by the light. A bandpass optical filter is used at the detector side, which eliminates all light except the light with the wavelength that the target gas molecules can absorb, which provides the specificity to the target gas against other gases that might be present in the volume probed by the IR light. This principle provides the basis for
