VII. CONCLUSION
The development of the IoT-based gas concentration monitoring systems is helping to better monitor the air quality. However, to keep the cost low for sensor deployment, one often uses sensors with components that suffer from unknown dependencies on environmental factors. These
unknown dependencies between the environmental factors and the sensor components lead to lower accuracy. Thus, with additional calibration, better performance of the whole gas monitoring system can be achieved. In this article, we developed several self-calibration algorithms for IoT-based gas concentration monitoring systems. The measurement errors of the sensors in the system are mainly caused by the remaining model errors that can be fully described by the drift of the calibration parameter. This leads to our first formulation of a statistical inference problem on the true calibration parameter under the HMM framework. When considering the networked calibration for the whole IoT-based system, the proposed weighted average belief function fusion approach can be used to deal with the case when belief functions provided by different sensors highly conflict with each other. The corresponding fusion result can be used as an approximation of the joint statistics when it is difficult to form a joint statistics among different sensors. As a consequence, the proposed POMDP-based multisensor sequential self-calibration approach utilizes the fusion result as an approximation of the joint statistics, and achieves more stable long-term calibration performance. Based on the numerical experiments, the weighted average belief function fusion approach is shown to have higher calibration accuracy than the POMDP-based approach when the majority of the sensors beliefs provide reliable and correct evidence. On the other hand, the POMDP-based approach can achieve mild but stable calibration accuracy over a relatively long period compared to the weighted average belief function fusion approach. Since there will be remaining model errors that cannot be fully captured by the proposed, every calibrated model will become out-of-date at some point of time. Future work is therefore to identify the optimal frequency a model should be updated and how this can be done efficiently, e.g., using a time-adaptive model.
